{
  "projection_type": "axonometric",
  "camera_view": { "azimuthDeg": 45, "elevationDeg": 30, "rollDeg": 0, "heightFraction": 0.62 },
  "elements": [
    "thin rectangular wireframe bounding box enclosing the whole plot (no tick marks, no axis labels, no numbers)",
    "broad flat light-blue sheet spanning the full box footprint at mid-height, representing V = 0 far from the charges",
    "steep narrow funnel-shaped peak rising from the RIGHT half of the sheet (positive charge)",
    "matching funnel-shaped depression plunging below the sheet on the LEFT half (negative charge)",
    "flat circular truncation cap cutting off the tip of the peak (sphere-surface clipping)",
    "flat circular truncation floor cutting off the bottom of the pit at the same depth",
    "height-mapped color banding on the funnel flanks: pale blue on the flat sheet grading to pink then violet near the extremes",
    "dark closed contour lines drawn on the surface, forming tight concentric rings on the steep flanks of both funnels",
    "wide rounded contour loops spreading out across the flat sheet away from the charges",
    "faint straight line crossing the flat sheet between the peak and the pit (the V = 0 symmetry line / midplane trace)",
    "vertical wireframe box corner edges visible in front of and behind the surface"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "orbitView",
      "type": "orbit",
      "teaches": "The hill and the trough are two halves of one continuous potential surface; rotating shows the pit is as deep as the peak is tall and that both decay smoothly into the flat far-field sheet."
    },
    {
      "id": "chargeBalance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Charge balance  q₋ / q₊",
      "range": [-2.0, 0.0, 0.05],
      "default": -1.0,
      "teaches": "Potential is a signed scalar: the sign of each charge decides whether the surface rises or falls, and the magnitude sets how tall the peak or how deep the well is. At -1 the figure is perfectly antisymmetric; away from -1 the flat sheet tilts because the net charge no longer cancels."
    },
    {
      "id": "chargeSeparation",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Charge separation d",
      "range": [0.8, 4.5, 0.1],
      "default": 2.5,
      "teaches": "As the equal and opposite charges are brought together the hill and trough overlap and partially cancel, flattening the region between them; pulled apart, each funnel becomes an isolated point-charge potential with its own concentric contours."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Representation",
      "options": ["Surface", "Surface + contours", "Contour map (top-down)"],
      "default": "Surface + contours",
      "teaches": "Contour loops are just the level curves of the height surface: where the surface is steep the contours crowd together, and crowded contours mean a large potential gradient, i.e. a strong electric field."
    },
    {
      "id": "probeHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Reading a single point off the surface gives the local potential value and shows how it falls as 1/r from each sphere, with the superposed value being the simple sum of the two contributions."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Equal and opposite charges",
      "narration": "A single smooth surface: potential rises into a steep peak over the positive sphere on the right and drops into an identical well over the negative sphere on the left, flattening to zero far away.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -1.0, "chargeSeparation": 2.5, "viewMode": "Surface" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Contours read the slope",
      "narration": "Adding level curves shows the loops packed tightly on the steep flanks and spread wide out on the flat sheet — close contours mark a strong field, wide ones a weak field.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -1.0, "chargeSeparation": 2.5, "viewMode": "Surface + contours" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Flatten to the contour map",
      "narration": "Looking straight down, the relief collapses into the familiar equipotential map: nested loops around each sphere and a straight zero-potential line down the middle.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -1.0, "chargeSeparation": 2.5, "viewMode": "Contour map (top-down)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Unbalance the charges",
      "narration": "Weakening the negative charge shrinks its well and lifts the far-field sheet above zero — the net charge no longer cancels, and the zero line bends around the smaller charge.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -0.4, "chargeSeparation": 2.5, "viewMode": "Surface + contours" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bring them together",
      "narration": "Sliding the spheres close, the peak and well overlap and largely cancel, leaving a compact dipole with a fast-collapsing potential and contours that close in tightly around the pair.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -1.0, "chargeSeparation": 1.0, "viewMode": "Surface + contours" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pull them apart",
      "narration": "Widely separated, each funnel becomes an essentially isolated point-charge potential with its own circular contours, and the region between goes nearly flat.",
      "state": { "chargeBalance": -1.0, "chargeSeparation": 4.0, "viewMode": "Surface + contours" }
    }
  ],
  "geometry_notes": "Domain: x ∈ [-3, 3] (screen-right), y ∈ [-3, 3] (receding depth), V plotted on the vertical axis z ∈ [-1.5, 1.5]. Bounding wireframe box is therefore 6 × 6 × 3 world units, drawn as thin gray (#909090) lines, ~1px, with the box wider than tall as in the source (roughly 1.6:1 screen aspect). The flat V = 0 sheet sits exactly at box mid-height, spanning the full 6 × 6 footprint. Potential: V(x,y) = k[ q₊ / max(r₊, a) + q₋ / max(r₋, a) ] with k = 1, q₊ = +1, q₋ = chargeBalance × 1, sphere radius a = 0.32, r± = sqrt((x ∓ d/2)² + y²). Charges sit on the x-axis at (+d/2, 0) POSITIVE = right-hand peak and (−d/2, 0) NEGATIVE = left-hand pit, with default d = 2.5 (so +1.25 and −1.25). Surface is hard-clipped at z = ±1.5, producing the flat circular cap on the peak tip and the flat circular floor in the pit, both ~0.5 units across — these truncations are visible in the source and must be kept. Left funnel in the source reads slightly smaller/further away than the right peak purely because of the viewing angle; keep them mathematically identical. Colors sampled from source: flat sheet / low relief light cornflower blue #8FC0EA to #A9D4F5; mid-flank pink #EFA6C8; steepest near-tip violet/purple #9C6FD2 to #7F5BC0; contour lines near-black #1A1A28 at ~0.8px, drawn as level sets of V at even spacing (about 12–16 levels over the full range) so they naturally crowd on the flanks; wireframe box #8A8A8A; background white. The faint straight line crossing the sheet between the two features is the V = 0 trace at x = 0 (plus the box's mid grid line) — draw it as a thin ~#6E7EA0 line running along the depth direction. Grid the surface at least 160 × 160 so the funnel walls stay smooth.",
  "notes": "Build the surface as a single indexed BufferGeometry over the (x,y) grid with per-vertex color from a height ramp (blue → pink → violet), MeshLambert/MeshStandard with flatShading off, side = DoubleSide so the pit interior reads correctly. Clamp z to ±1.5 per vertex, which automatically yields the flat truncation caps; optionally add explicit circular disks at the clip planes so the caps are opaque. Contours: compute marching-squares polylines on the same scalar grid per frame and render as LineSegments offset ~0.005 along the surface normal (or use polygonOffset) to avoid z-fighting. For the top-down contour mode, tween the camera to elevation 90° and tween all z-values toward 0 rather than swapping objects, so the learner sees the relief flatten into the loops. Regenerate vertex positions/colors in place when either slider changes — do not rebuild geometry. Box is a LineSegments wireframe, no ticks or labels. Soft directional key light from upper-left plus ambient so the violet flanks stay readable; no shadows."
}

