CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a single three-dimensional surface plot enclosed in a thin rectangular wireframe box, with no axis labels, tick marks, or numerical scales shown. A broad flat blue sheet spans the box at mid-height, standing for the level far from the charges. On the right half of the sheet the surface rises into a steep, narrow peak whose flanks are banded in pink and violet and whose tip is cut off flat. On the left half the surface plunges into a matching funnel-shaped depression, shaded the same way and likewise truncated at its base. Dark contour lines run across the surface as closed loops encircling both the peak and the pit, crowding together where the surface steepens and spreading into wide rounded loops out on the flat sheet, with a faint straight line crossing the sheet between the two features. Rendering potential as height converts the sign and size of the potential into visible relief, so the hill and the trough can be read against each other directly. The plot builds intuition for the potential of two equal and opposite charges carried on conducting spheres of finite radius.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - thin rectangular wireframe bounding box enclosing the whole plot (no tick marks, no axis labels, no numbers)
  - broad flat light-blue sheet spanning the full box footprint at mid-height, representing V = 0 far from the charges
  - steep narrow funnel-shaped peak rising from the RIGHT half of the sheet (positive charge)
  - matching funnel-shaped depression plunging below the sheet on the LEFT half (negative charge)
  - flat circular truncation cap cutting off the tip of the peak (sphere-surface clipping)
  - flat circular truncation floor cutting off the bottom of the pit at the same depth
  - height-mapped color banding on the funnel flanks: pale blue on the flat sheet grading to pink then violet near the extremes
  - dark closed contour lines drawn on the surface, forming tight concentric rings on the steep flanks of both funnels
  - wide rounded contour loops spreading out across the flat sheet away from the charges
  - faint straight line crossing the flat sheet between the peak and the pit (the V = 0 symmetry line / midplane trace)
  - vertical wireframe box corner edges visible in front of and behind the surface

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Domain: x ∈ [-3, 3] (screen-right), y ∈ [-3, 3] (receding depth), V plotted on the vertical axis z ∈ [-1.5, 1.5]. Bounding wireframe box is therefore 6 × 6 × 3 world units, drawn as thin gray (#909090) lines, ~1px, with the box wider than tall as in the source (roughly 1.6:1 screen aspect). The flat V = 0 sheet sits exactly at box mid-height, spanning the full 6 × 6 footprint. Potential: V(x,y) = k[ q₊ / max(r₊, a) + q₋ / max(r₋, a) ] with k = 1, q₊ = +1, q₋ = chargeBalance × 1, sphere radius a = 0.32, r± = sqrt((x ∓ d/2)² + y²). Charges sit on the x-axis at (+d/2, 0) POSITIVE = right-hand peak and (−d/2, 0) NEGATIVE = left-hand pit, with default d = 2.5 (so +1.25 and −1.25). Surface is hard-clipped at z = ±1.5, producing the flat circular cap on the peak tip and the flat circular floor in the pit, both ~0.5 units across — these truncations are visible in the source and must be kept. Left funnel in the source reads slightly smaller/further away than the right peak purely because of the viewing angle; keep them mathematically identical. Colors sampled from source: flat sheet / low relief light cornflower blue #8FC0EA to #A9D4F5; mid-flank pink #EFA6C8; steepest near-tip violet/purple #9C6FD2 to #7F5BC0; contour lines near-black #1A1A28 at ~0.8px, drawn as level sets of V at even spacing (about 12–16 levels over the full range) so they naturally crowd on the flanks; wireframe box #8A8A8A; background white. The faint straight line crossing the sheet between the two features is the V = 0 trace at x = 0 (plus the box's mid grid line) — draw it as a thin ~#6E7EA0 line running along the depth direction. Grid the surface at least 160 × 160 so the funnel walls stay smooth.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
